d we need  to go in and study the entire tissue  repository that is a  thing that no one can know government  grant can support hundred million  dollars to one individual and I don't  need the money to set up you know a  building with 70 scientists working in  it no this is money that's needed to pay  for the actual tests for the samples to  do the proteomics on two hundred  patients  cost four million dollars this is the  kind of that's that's where the money  will go so the other idea that occurred  to me was if there are any mindscape  listeners out there who would like to  donate a hundred million dollars the  tissue repository will encourage them to  do that I mean can you just I'd like to  try to give the the audience a sort of  visceral feel for what it's like to be  doing this kind of work number one let  me just mention this kind of  longitudinal study is the gold standard  in science and social sciences  especially and really hard and expensive  to do because you have to start young  and continue to be dedicated and get the  resources but how like where is it where  are these tissues like are they in a  room somewhere they kept cold or they do  do they still duplicate the cells or is  there a finite number of cells that we  use up so it's then tied tissue  repository is housed at Columbia  University Cancer Center it moved with  me from Buffalo to Cincinnati to Chicago  to Massachusetts to New York now and it  is since 1984 which travelled with me  and these are samples which are in ref  in freezers but in addition we have  frozen viable tissue also which can be  brought out and re grown in plates and  animals in vitro in vivo so it's an  immense collection of samples everything  belongs to Columbia University I don't  have any companies to my name I don't  need any personal money this is all  going to go through Columbia University  so one thing that occurred to me Sean is  that the people who should be most  interested in finding the first cell are  the 